Day 10 — Jenkins and SonarQube Code Quality in CI CD


1. Why Code Quality in CI/CD?

	Code quality is crucial in CI/CD pipelines because:

	Shift-left testing: Catch issues early in development

	Automated enforcement: Prevent bad code from reaching production

	Technical debt reduction: Address issues incrementally

	Team standards: Ensure consistency across developers

	Security: Identify vulnerabilities before deployment



2. SonarQube Overview

What is SonarQube?

	An open-source platform for continuous inspection of code quality

Key Features:

	Static code analysis for 30+ languages

	Real-time feedback on code quality

	Track technical debt over time

	Pull request analysis (in commercial versions)


SonarQube Architecture:

[Source Code] → [SonarScanner] → [SonarQube Server] → [Dashboard/Reports]


What is SonarScanner?

A client application that analyzes code and sends data to SonarQube

Common Usage:


# Using sonar-scanner CLI

sonar-scanner \
  -Dsonar.projectKey=my_project \
  -Dsonar.sources=. \
  -Dsonar.host.url=http://localhost:9000 \
  -Dsonar.login=myauthenticationtoken


4. Key Quality Metrics


Definition: Coding errors that will break code

Example: Null pointer dereference, infinite loops

Severity levels: Blocker, Critical, Major, Minor, Info

----------------------------------------------------------

Vulnerabilities

Definition: Security weaknesses that can be exploited

Example: SQL injection, hardcoded passwords, XSS

OWASP Top 10: Most common vulnerabilities tracked

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Code Smells

Definition: Maintainability issues (not bugs, but problematic)

Example: Duplicated code, overly complex methods, long classes

Impact: Increases technical debt


-----------------------------------------------------------------


Test Coverage

Definition: Percentage of code covered by tests

Metrics:

Line coverage

Branch coverage

Condition coverage


--------------------------------------------------------------

Duplications

Definition: Duplicated code blocks

Threshold: Usually >3% duplicate code is problematic


---------------------------------------------------------------


Technical Debt


Definition: Estimated time to fix all issues

Formula: Technical Debt = (Remediation cost × Issues)



----------------------------------------------------------------


5. Quality Gates

What are Quality Gates?

	Predefined criteria that code must meet to pass quality standards

Built-in Quality Gates:

	SonarQube way: Default gate with balanced metrics

	SonarQube way (latest): Updated version

	Security-focused: Higher security standards


----------------------------------------------------------------------

Common Quality Gate Conditions:


# Example Quality Gate Conditions

- Bugs:
    New Bugs: 0
    Overall Bugs: < 10

- Vulnerabilities:
    New Vulnerabilities: 0
    Overall Vulnerabilities: < 5

- Code Coverage:
    New Coverage: > 80%
    Overall Coverage: > 70%

- Duplications:
    New Duplications: < 3%
    Overall Duplications: < 5%

- Code Smells:
    New Code Smells: < 10
    Overall Code Smells: < 100

- Security Rating: A or B

- Maintainability Rating: A or B